  "hints": [
    "Divisors come in pairs d and num//d.",
    "Inspect d only through the integer square root and add each partner once.",
    "Reject 1; start the sum at 1; for every divisor from 2 through isqrt(num), add it and its distinct partner; compare with num.",
    "Do not add num itself, and do not count the square-root divisor twice for a square."
  ],
  "editorial": "Every non-unit proper divisor pair has one member at most sqrt(num). This lets us enumerate all pairs without scanning up to num. A square-root pair contributes only once. The small oracle literally tests every positive integer below num; a separate prime-factor divisor-sum certificate checks large fixtures.",
